{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792245251,"nonce":"kU5GJ5Waufjj2eLuSNCv9um2AdE=","title":"AOT emitted code for a generic CPU through the system opt/llc (nbody 5x slower than the JIT)","message":"When opt-21/llc-21 were on PATH, `rayzor aot` printed the module as IR text and ran the system tools with no target CPU (x86-64 baseline: no FMA3/AVX2) and with the nnan/ninf/nsz flags stripped. The path predates the in-process LLVM being 21; it was added when the bundled LLVM was 18.\n\nNUC (i5-1250P), cold, fresh process, -O3, ms (hxcpp in brackets):\n- nbody: system tools 2106, in-process 363 (413)\n- spectralnorm: 668 -\u003e 154 (163)\n- every other benchmark within noise\n\nThe in-process path compiles for the host CPU and gives the same mandelbrot checksum as the JIT (112798288, inside the contraction band).\n\nFix: always use the in-process LLVM (MIR at the requested level, LLVM default\u003cOn\u003e, main() wrapper added after optimization). Verified: AOT regression checks 6/6, benchmark outputs match hxcpp, old-vs-new AOT over the Haxe suite matched on 49 of the 50 tests that build (the other differs only in a timing-dependent spin count), workspace clippy clean.\n\nFollow-up (user): thread the requested target triple through the whole AOT path as Ash does: triple and data layout on the module before lowering, host CPU only when the triple is the host's, and a target runtime archive when cross-compiling.","files":null}]}